@page
@model MigrationModel
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Migration"; }

<div class="crumbs"><a href="/Dashboard">Home</a> › Migration</div>

<div class="pagehead">
    <div>
        <h1>Migration Trace</h1>
        <div class="sub">What each part of this app came from in the OutSystems module</div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="prov">
    <strong>Read this before using the app as evidence.</strong>
    A running app does not prove the OML to XML extraction was faithful. This app was written
    from the extracted XML, so anything the extraction dropped would simply be absent here,
    and the app would still run. What it does show is that the XML is complete enough to
    rebuild working behaviour from, and that the data model and business rules in it hold
    together. The real corroboration is the screenshot comparison in the last section.
</div>

<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:16px">
    <h2>The one SQL node</h2>
    <p class="muted">
        The source module has a single hand-written SQL node. In the extracted XML it is not
        stored as a string: it is a <code>ParsedSQLExpression</code> split across dozens of
        <code>Text</code> and <code>Unbound</code> elements, with table and parameter names held
        as key references. Reassembling those fragments in order produces this:
    </p>
    <pre class="sql">@ExpensesManager.Services.ReportingService.OriginalSqlServer</pre>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:18px">
        Running here against SQLite, with <code>DATEADD</code> and <code>GETDATE</code> translated
        and the as-of date passed in because the sample data is from 2024:
    </p>
    <pre class="sql">@ExpensesManager.Services.ReportingService.MigratedSqlite</pre>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:18px">
        This query returned <strong>@Model.SqlRowCount rows</strong> just now, and feeds the
        "Expenses &amp; Earnings per date" chart on the dashboard.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:16px">
    <h2>Element mapping</h2>
    <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width:30%">OutSystems</th>
                <th style="width:34%">Source element</th>
                <th>Here</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var m in Model.Mappings)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>@m.Concept</strong></td>
                    <td class="muted"><code>@m.Source</code></td>
                    <td>@m.Target</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="card" style="margin-bottom:16px">
    <h2>Static Entity values, as extracted</h2>
    <p class="muted">
        These are design-time records from the module, not table data. OutSystems logic refers
        to them by name, so the Id values are effectively constants that the code depends on.
        They are seeded into this database unchanged.
    </p>
    <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Entity</th><th>Id</th><th>Label</th><th>Order</th><th>Active</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var s in Model.StaticRecords)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td class="muted">@s.Entity</td>
                    <td>@s.Id</td>
                    <td><strong>@s.Label</strong></td>
                    <td>@s.Order</td>
                    <td>@(s.Active ? "Yes" : "No")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px">
        Note <code>Draft</code> and <code>Submitted</code> both carry Order 2. That duplicate is
        in the source app and was reproduced rather than corrected.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>What actually corroborates the extraction</h2>
    <p class="muted">
        The original hosted demo is gone, so the screenshots in the legacy repo are the only
        surviving record of the app running. These values were read off those screenshots and
        compared against what came out of the binary modules:
    </p>
    <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
            <tr><th style="width:38%">Checked</th><th style="width:31%">In the screenshots</th><th>Extracted independently</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var c in Model.Corroboration)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>@c.What</strong></td>
                    <td class="muted">@c.Screenshot</td>
                    <td>@c.Extracted</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:16px">
        The dashboard figures in this build were reconstructed to land on the original's numbers,
        so treat the first three rows as the real evidence: those labels and the report code format
        came out of the binary before any screenshot was consulted.
    </p>
</div>
